# LUCI visual options workflow — 3 variants on 3 branches

**When to use this:** whenever a task involves a **visual / layout / design decision** where the direction isn't already locked — e.g. a new section, hero, page layout, brochure/cover page, header treatment, diagram composition, industry page, case-study hero, email masthead, or any "let's try a new direction" request.

**When NOT to use this (just do the thing):** tiny tweaks, copy edits, color/spacing refinements, bug fixes, deploys, content swaps, or any change where the user has already specified the exact direction. Don't spawn variants for a one-line fix.

## The default offer

Before deep implementation of an open-ended visual decision, **proactively offer** (don't silently do it, don't silently skip it):

> "This is a visual direction call — want me to build 2–3 variants on separate branches so you can compare live, then we merge the winner?"

If the user says yes (or "go ahead"), run the variant workflow below. If they say "just do one" or specify the direction, do that instead.

## The variant workflow

1. **Start from a clean `main`** (commit current work first — see `luci-commit-cadence.mdc` if present; otherwise commit a quick checkpoint so branches branch from a known-good state).
2. **Create 2–3 branches**, one per direction, named `<task>-variant-a`, `<task>-variant-b`, `<task>-variant-c` (e.g. `brochure-p1-variant-a`). Make each direction genuinely different in the *right* axis (layout density, hierarchy, accent placement, tone) — not three near-identical polish passes.
3. **Build each variant** on its own branch. For parallel speed, use the `best-of-n-runner` subagent (one per branch) when the variants are independent.
4. **Deploy/preview each** to its review surface (website → `./deploy.sh` → `http://10.10.1.37`; portal → `npm run deploy:portal` → `http://10.10.1.17:8081`; design preview → `localhost:8765`). If a single review surface can't show three branches at once, screenshot each and post the three images side by side with labels A/B/C and the branch name on each.
5. **Present them clearly:** "A = <direction>, B = <direction>, C = <direction>. Which way?" Let the user pick.
6. **Merge the winner** into `main` (fast-forward or a clean merge), then **delete the losing branches** (`git branch -D`). Don't leave dead variant branches lying around.
7. **Commit the merge** with a message that names the chosen direction.

## Why branches (not one file edited three times)

Branches mean `main` stays clean and every variant is independently reviewable and recoverable. If a direction is wrong, `git checkout main` and it's gone — no manual undo, no "you changed the actual design, revert" friction. This only works because work is committed often; if `main` has weeks of uncommitted changes, branching is meaningless. Commit first, branch second.

## Respect the user's time

- Two variants is fine when the axis is binary (e.g. "dense vs. airy"). Three when there's room to explore.
- Don't spend hours on throwaway variants — these are direction probes, not final polish. Keep each one buildable in minutes, not a full production pass.
- If the user has already pasted a reference image or said "like X," that's a direction — build one variant in that direction, not three random ones.
